---
// Work with us. Second of the four "Meet the team" pages
// (src/data/meetTheTeam.ts), and the only page on this site whose reader is
// not a client. Everything about it follows from that: the trust bar is the
// employment one rather than the client one, the CTA is a conversation
// rather than a quote, and the proof it needs is about how the team is
// treated rather than how the cleaning turns out.
//
// NO CONTENT PLAN EXISTS FOR THIS PAGE, and unlike /about/ it has almost no
// published facts to build on either. Its foundations are:
//   1. "TLB Cleaning - Brand Foundation v2.0.md" §1.2, §1.3 and §1.5.6, which are
//      explicit that flexible, family-first hours are the employment model
//      and the reason the team stays.
//   2. why-tlb.astro §8, the same argument written for clients.
//   3. index.astro's v6 FAQ, for what the business already tells clients
//      about equipment and cover.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH. Four things on this page are statements about a real
// employment relationship, and a careers page that gets one wrong is worse
// than no careers page:
//  1. THE HIRING PROCESS IS A PROPOSAL. Nothing in the repo or the brand
//     foundation describes how TLB actually hires. The three steps below are
//     written as a sensible default and marked as such here. Confirm them
//     with Teagan or cut the section.
//  2. PAY IS NOT ON THE PAGE, deliberately. There is no rate, band or award
//     reference anywhere in the repo. Do not let anyone fill it with a guess
//     or a "competitive rates" line, which tells an applicant nothing and
//     reads as evasive. Name the award and the band once confirmed.
//  3. THE TRANSPORT AND EQUIPMENT CONDITIONS are asserted below (own
//     transport between properties, gear and products supplied). The second
//     is what index.astro already tells clients. The first is not published
//     anywhere. Confirm both.
//  4. THE EMPLOYEE CLAIM. This page tells applicants they are employed
//     rather than engaged under an ABN, and the callout explains the
//     difference to them. That has to be true of every hire, including
//     casuals, or the page misleads the person it is recruiting.
//
// ⚠️ Every CTA here points at /contact/, which DOES NOT EXIST. primaryNav
// has linked it sitewide since navigation.ts was written, so the 404 is
// pre-existing rather than introduced here, but this is the first page whose
// only conversion route is that link. An applicant cannot use "get an
// instant quote".
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import TextBlock from '../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import ContentGrid from '../components/sections/ContentGrid.astro';
import Callout from '../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import TagCloud from '../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import Faq from '../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
import footerLogoImg from '../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
// Hero: two cleaners working a kitchen together, generated for this page.
// The subject is deliberately the pair rather than one person alone, because
// the offer on this page is a team and a set run, not shift work.
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../data/navigation';
import { locationGroups } from '../data/locations';
import { meetTheTeamBusiness, otherMeetTheTeamPages } from '../data/meetTheTeam';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../lib/cms-schemas';

// The applicant's CTA, used in the hero and again at the close. Deliberately
// NOT the sitewide quoteCta, which asks the reader to price a clean they do
// not want. The label is the brand foundation's own preferred CTA (§2.4).

// The employment-side trust bar, not the client one. src/data/trust.ts holds
// five client proof points ("98% of our clients stay with us", "Over 100
// local homes, hosts and businesses") and every one of them answers a
// question an applicant is not asking. Using it here would be the drift that
// file was extracted to prevent, in reverse.
//
// `divided` rather than `card`: three short phrases in a hairline row, which
// is what that variant is for. The card variant overlaps the hero and is the
// site's client-facing signature, so keeping it off this page also keeps the
// two audiences visually distinct.
//
// ⚠️ All three are employment claims. See blockers 3 and 4 at the top.

// What the job is, before what it offers. An applicant who reads three
// paragraphs of benefits and then finds out on day one that the work is
// physical and clock-driven does not stay, which defeats the entire point of
// the model this page is selling.

// What the arrangement offers. Four cells, two columns, so the pairs read
// across. Written as things that either happen or do not, per the same rule
// /about/'s values grid follows.
//
// ⚠️ Cell 3 (gear and products supplied) is what index.astro's v6 FAQ
// already tells clients, so it is consistent. Cell 4 (training against a
// written standard) is NOT published anywhere and is the strongest claim in
// this section. Confirm it exists before launch, because an applicant will
// arrive expecting it.

// What we ask, as a short list between two lines of prose, which is exactly
// why TextBlock's `body` is polymorphic (see its own note on that).
//
// ⚠️ The transport line is a real condition of employment and it is not
// published anywhere in the repo. If TLB has team members who work a single
// town on foot, this sentence turns them away for no reason.

// The employment-model callout. This page's equivalent of why-tlb.astro's
// contract-literacy section, pointed at an applicant instead of a client:
// the same "here is what to check before you sign" move, which is the one
// piece of genuinely useful writing a cleaning careers page can offer.
//
// tone="note" rather than "warning": it is worth knowing rather than don't
// do this. The thing being warned against is somebody else's arrangement,
// and telling an applicant that a contracting offer is a mistake is not
// ours to say.

// The work we take on, staff-facing. Icon-grid rather than the `list`
// variant this site usually reaches for, because these are not links: an
// applicant is reading what the days look like, not choosing a service to
// buy. Icons are the four that exist in ServiceIcon.astro and actually mean
// something here.

// ⚠️ THE THREE STEPS BELOW ARE A PROPOSAL, NOT TLB'S PROCESS. See blocker 1
// at the top of this file. They are written as the default a small local
// employer would actually run, and they are specific on purpose: a vague
// "we will be in touch" is what makes people not bother applying. Confirm or
// replace, and if step 3 is paid, say so, because that is the detail an
// applicant is weighing.
//
// Numbered in the headings rather than with a numbered component:
// PathwayCards is the numbered one and it needs a photo and a CTA per card,
// neither of which a three-step process has.

// FAQs, applicant-facing. Answers 1, 3, 4 and 5 restate conditions stated
// elsewhere on this page or already published to clients. Answer 6 is
// deliberately hedged, because where the next run opens depends on where the
// work is, and promising an applicant a job in their town would be the one
// dishonest sentence on the page.
//
// ⚠️ "What does it pay?" IS NOT HERE. See blocker 2. It is the first thing
// an applicant wants and the one thing this repo cannot supply. Add it as
// soon as there is an award and a band to name.

// Cross-links to the other three pages in this cluster. An applicant reading
// this page is also, quietly, checking whether the business is real, and
// /about/ and /reviews/ are where they go to do that.
const clusterItems = otherMeetTheTeamPages('/work-with-us/');

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/work-with-us.yaml and is edited in Pages
// CMS ("Work with us"). This file owns the layout. The notes above explain why
// each section says what it says; they stay here because Pages CMS rewrites
// the YAML on save and would drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'work-with-us',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    // The applicant's button. The hero and the closing section use it unless
    // their own button is filled in.
    applyCta: section.link,
    hero: section.hero,
    // The `divided` trust bar: one short phrase per item, this page's own.
    trustBar: z.object({ items: z.array(section.text).min(1) }),
    theJob: section.textBlock,
    offers: section.textGrid,
    asks: section.textBlock,
    abn: section.callout,
    workTypes: section.iconGrid,
    howToApply: section.textGrid,
    whereWeWork: section.tagCloud,
    faq: section.faq,
    checkingUs: section.sectionIntro,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/work-with-us/',
};

const business = meetTheTeamBusiness(pageMeta.description);

const applyCta = page.applyCta;

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '4/3');

const offerBlocks = page.offers.items.map((item) => ({
  type: 'text' as const,
  ...item,
  tone: 'muted' as const,
}));

const processBlocks = page.howToApply.items.map((item) => ({ type: 'text' as const, ...item }));

---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? applyCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    <TrustBar items={page.trustBar.items} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.theJob.heading}
      body={page.theJob.body}
      cta={page.theJob.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.theJob.backgroundImage}
    />

    <ContentGrid heading={page.offers.heading} lead={page.offers.lead} columns={2} blocks={offerBlocks} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.asks.heading}
      body={page.asks.body}
      cta={page.asks.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.asks.backgroundImage}
    />

    <Callout tone="note" heading={page.abn.heading} body={page.abn.body} cta={page.abn.cta} />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="icon-grid"
      heading={page.workTypes.heading}
      lead={page.workTypes.lead}
      note={page.workTypes.note}
      items={page.workTypes.items}
    />

    <ContentGrid heading={page.howToApply.heading} lead={page.howToApply.lead} columns={3} blocks={processBlocks} />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeWork.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeWork.subheading}
      tags={page.whereWeWork.tags}
      groups={locationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeWork.note}
    />

    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={page.faq.items} />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="list"
      heading={page.checkingUs.heading}
      lead={page.checkingUs.lead}
      items={clusterItems}
    />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? applyCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
